<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick I, Elector of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1370">1370</a>–<a href="/years/1428">1428</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Frederick the Warlike · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick I, called the Warlike, was a Wettin margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> who became the first Wettin Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. He founded the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1409, and in 1423 Emperor Sigismund granted him the Duchy of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> with its electoral title as a reward for his support against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a>. His heirs ruled Saxony until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-i-elector-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Frederick I, Elector of Saxony" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_I._von_Sachsen,_1370-1428_(AT_KHM_GG4792).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born on 11 April 1370, the eldest son of Frederick III, Landgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, and Catherine of Henneberg. After the death of his uncle William I, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, in 1407, he became governor of the Margraviate of Meissen together with his brother William II and his cousin Frederick IV, the son of Balthasar, until their possessions were divided in 1410 and 1415. On 7 February 1402 he had married Catherine of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, daughter of Henry the Mild, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg; they had seven children, among them Frederick II, who succeeded him as elector.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Frederick took part in several wars before his elevation. In the German town war of 1388 he assisted Frederick V of Hohenzollern, burgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, and in 1391 he helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> against Wladislaus II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He supported Rupert III, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, in his struggle with King Wenceslaus for the German throne, probably because Wenceslaus had refused to give him his sister Anna in marriage. In 1409 he and his brother William founded the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> for German students who had left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-university">University of Prague</a> after the events relating to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a>.</p><p>The danger from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> led him to ally with Emperor Sigismund, and he took a leading part in the war against them, with considerable success in its early years. In this campaign he spent large sums and received places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and elsewhere in pledge from Sigismund, who rewarded him on 6 January 1423 with the vacant electoral Duchy of Saxony-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>. His formal investiture followed at Ofen on 1 August 1425. On 16 August 1426 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> army was almost annihilated at Aussig, while he was trying to rouse the German princes to join the war. After the death of his brother William he ruled all the possessions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> in 1428 and was buried as the first Wettin in the centre of what is now the Princes Chapel in Meissen Cathedral. Called the Belligerent or the Warlike, he secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> electorship for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a> and thereby established the dynasty's future importance in German politics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick I, Elector of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Ascanian electoral line died out with Albert III in 1422, Emperor Sigismund granted the electorate in 1423 to <b>Frederick the Warlike</b>, Margrave of Meissen of the House of Wettin, as a reward for his support against the Hussites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick I, Elector of Saxony</b>, and his brother William II, Margrave of Meissen, founded the university of Leipzig with the four scholastic faculties.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1423 Emperor Sigismund granted <b>Frederick the Warlike</b> the Duchy of Saxe-Wittenberg with its electoral dignity, after the Ascanian line died out.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-meissen">Margraviate of Meissen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1407" aria-label="Dresden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4862 394 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick I, Elector of Saxony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1407">What was here in 1407? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
